IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ucpr-dates`
IMPORT `ucpr-rules`

-- Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999 (Qld) -- the questions of the person bringing a claim in a Queensland court (the
-- Supreme Court, the District Court or a Magistrates Court): usually the plaintiff who is injured or owed money, and the
-- same person where the rules give them a step to answer (a reply, an answer to a counterclaim, a notice to admit).
--
-- Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. For the latest information on Queensland
-- Government legislation please go to https://www.legislation.qld.gov.au. (c) The State of Queensland, licensed under
-- CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts only. What the court or registrar decides (leave, satisfaction, another costs order)
-- is asked as a fact: "if the court ...". The court can extend any time set by the rules (r 7). Days are counted
-- without the day of the event; no last day is moved for a weekend or holiday here (Acts Interpretation Act 1954 s 38
-- is not deposited: see NOTES.md, fork F1).

§ `Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999 -- my questions as a claimant`

§§ `My claim: how long it lasts and renewing it (r 24)`

@export Until what day does my claim stay in force in a Queensland court (r 24(1) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? A claim remains in force for 1 year starting on the day it is filed, so its last day is the day before the first anniversary of filing.
GIVEN `the day the claim was filed` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your claim filed in the court registry (ISO date, e.g. 2026-03-15)?
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when is my claim in force?` `the day the claim was filed` MEANS
    `r 24(1) -- the last day my claim is in force` `the day the claim was filed`

@export Can my unserved claim be renewed in a Queensland court, and do I need the court's leave (r 24(2)-(4) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? The registrar may renew an unserved claim for up to a year at a time if satisfied reasonable efforts were made to serve it or there is another good reason; any period reaching the fifth anniversary of filing needs the court's leave.
GIVEN `the day the claim was originally filed` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your claim first filed (ISO date)?
      `the day the claim would otherwise end` IS A DATE @desc What is the last day your claim is now in force (the day before the anniversary of filing, or the last day of the last renewal), ISO date?
      `the claim has been served on the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the claim already been served on the defendant?
      `the registrar is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the registrar satisfied that you made reasonable efforts to serve the defendant, or that there is another good reason to renew the claim?
GIVETH A `Renewal answer`
`Can my claim be renewed?` `the day the claim was originally filed` `the day the claim would otherwise end` `the claim has been served on the defendant` `the registrar is satisfied` MEANS
    `r 24(2)-(4) -- can my claim be renewed` `the day the claim was originally filed` `the day the claim would otherwise end` `the claim has been served on the defendant` `the registrar is satisfied`

@export For a limitation period or other time limit, on what day is my renewed claim taken to have started (r 24(6) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? On the day it was originally filed, however often it has been renewed.
GIVEN `the day the claim was originally filed` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your claim first filed (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`When is my renewed claim taken to have started?` `the day the claim was originally filed` MEANS
    `r 24(6) -- the day my renewed claim is taken to have started` `the day the claim was originally filed`

§§ `The defendant's notice of intention to defend, and default judgment (rr 137, 281-288, 283(2), 694)`

@export By when must the defendant file a notice of intention to defend my claim (r 137 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 28 days after the day the claim is served, unless the Service and Execution of Process Act 1992 (Cwlth) applies.
GIVEN `the day the claim was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your claim served on the defendant (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant file a notice of intention to defend?` `the day the claim was served` MEANS
    `r 137 -- the last day for the notice of intention to defend` `the day the claim was served`

@export Can I get a default judgment against a defendant who has not filed a notice of intention to defend my claim, and what kind (rr 281-288 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Only once the 28 days have ended with no notice filed and I can prove service; then by the kind of claim: a debt, damages to be assessed, goods, land, or an application to the court.
GIVEN `the day the claim was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your claim served on the defendant (ISO date)?
      `the day I request judgment` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you file the request or application for judgment (ISO date)?
      `the Service and Execution of Process Act applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the claim served outside Queensland in Australia, so that the Service and Execution of Process Act 1992 (Cwlth) sets the time to defend?
      `a notice of intention to defend has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant filed a notice of intention to defend at any time before your request (even late)?
      `I can prove service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you prove the claim was served on the defendant (for example by an affidavit of service)?
      `my claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What is your claim against this defendant? One of: "a debt or liquidated demand" (a fixed sum owed), "unliquidated damages" (compensation to be assessed, e.g. for an injury), "detention of goods only", "recovery of possession of land only", "two or more of those four kinds and nothing else", "any other claim".
      `possession is claimed under a mortgage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you claim possession of land, is it delivery of possession under a mortgage?
GIVETH A `Default judgment answer`
`Can I get default judgment?` `the day the claim was served` `the day I request judgment` `the Service and Execution of Process Act applies` `a notice of intention to defend has been filed` `I can prove service` `my claim` `possession is claimed under a mortgage` MEANS
    `rr 281-288 -- can I get default judgment` `the day the claim was served` `the day I request judgment` `the Service and Execution of Process Act applies` `a notice of intention to defend has been filed` `I can prove service` `my claim` `possession is claimed under a mortgage`

@export What fixed costs can I include in a default judgment for a debt in a Queensland court (rr 283(2)(b) and 694 and Schedules 1 and 2 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? The scale costs for issuing the claim and obtaining judgment, plus fees and payments reasonably incurred and paid; in a Magistrates Court they depend on the amount, and over $50,000 there is no fixed figure.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc Which court is your claim in? One of: "the Supreme Court or the District Court", "a Magistrates Court".
      `the amount of the judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc How much is the judgment for, in Australian dollars?
      `fees and payments reasonably incurred and paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much have you reasonably paid in other fees and payments (e.g. filing and service fees), in Australian dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What fixed costs go on my default judgment?` `the court` `the amount of the judgment` `fees and payments reasonably incurred and paid` MEANS
    `r 283(2)(b) -- the fixed costs on my default judgment` `the court` `the amount of the judgment` `fees and payments reasonably incurred and paid`

§§ `Pleadings and admissions (rr 164, 169, 189)`

@export By when must I file and serve my reply to the defence (r 164(2) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 14 days after the day the defence was served, unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the day the defence was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the defence served on you (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I reply?` `the day the defence was served` MEANS
    `r 164(2) -- the last day for my reply` `the day the defence was served`

@export By when must I file and serve my answer to a counterclaim against me (r 164(1) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 14 days after it is served, or 28 days if I was not a party to the original proceeding, unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the day the counterclaim was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the counterclaim served on you (ISO date)?
      `I was a party to the original proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you already a party to the proceeding (e.g. the plaintiff) before the counterclaim?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I answer the counterclaim?` `the day the counterclaim was served` `I was a party to the original proceeding` MEANS
    `r 164(1) -- the last day for my answer to counterclaim` `the day the counterclaim was served` `I was a party to the original proceeding`

@export On what day do the pleadings in my case close (r 169 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? On service of a pleading served after the defence (such as my reply), otherwise 14 days after service of the defence.
GIVEN `the day the defence was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the defence served (ISO date)?
      `a pleading was served after the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a further pleading (such as your reply) served after the defence?
      `the day that pleading was served` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was it served (ISO date)? Otherwise give any date.
GIVETH A DATE
`When do the pleadings close?` `the day the defence was served` `a pleading was served after the defence` `the day that pleading was served` MEANS
    `r 169 -- the day the pleadings close` `the day the defence was served` `a pleading was served after the defence` `the day that pleading was served`

@export Is the other party taken to admit the facts or documents in my notice to admit (r 189 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Yes if it has not served a notice disputing them within 14 days; it can withdraw only with the court's leave.
GIVEN `the day my notice to admit was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your notice to admit served on the other party (ISO date)?
      `a disputing notice was served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the other party served a notice disputing the facts or documents?
      `the day the disputing notice was served` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was it served on you (ISO date)? Otherwise give any date.
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date (ISO date)?
GIVETH A `Notice to admit answer`
`Is the other party taken to admit?` `the day my notice to admit was served` `a disputing notice was served` `the day the disputing notice was served` `today` MEANS
    `r 189 -- is the other party taken to admit` `the day my notice to admit was served` `a disputing notice was served` `the day the disputing notice was served` `today`

§§ `Disclosure (r 214)`

@export By when must I deliver my list of documents to the other parties (r 214(2) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 28 days after the close of pleadings, unless an order for disclosure made before then sets the times (no answer then: the order governs).
GIVEN `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the pleadings close (ISO date)?
      `an order for disclosure was made before the close of pleadings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the court make an order for disclosure before the pleadings closed?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I deliver my list of documents?` `the day the pleadings closed` `an order for disclosure was made before the close of pleadings` MEANS
    `r 214(2) -- the last day for my list of documents` `the day the pleadings closed` `an order for disclosure was made before the close of pleadings`

@export By when must I disclose a document I find or obtain after my disclosure time has passed (r 214(2)(d) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 7 days after I get or locate it.
GIVEN `the day I found or got the document` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the document come into your possession or control, or did you locate it (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I disclose a later document?` `the day I found or got the document` MEANS
    `r 214(2)(d) -- the last day to disclose a later document` `the day I found or got the document`

@export By when must I deliver copies of listed documents another party has asked for (r 214(3) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 14 days after the request.
GIVEN `the day copies were requested` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the other party ask for copies (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I deliver requested copies?` `the day copies were requested` MEANS
    `r 214(3) -- the last day to deliver requested copies` `the day copies were requested`

§§ `Personal injury claims: statements of loss and damage (rr 545-558) -- only if my claim is for damages for personal injury or death`

@export Must I serve a statement of loss and damage in my Queensland personal injury claim (rr 545-547 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Yes in a proceeding for damages for personal injury or death, within 28 days after the close of pleadings and before a request for trial date, unless compliance was waived in writing.
GIVEN `my claim is for damages for personal injury or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your proceeding a claim for damages for personal injury or for a death?
      `the defendant waived compliance in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant waived further compliance with these rules by written notice?
GIVETH A `Statement answer`
`Must I serve a statement of loss and damage?` `my claim is for damages for personal injury or death` `the defendant waived compliance in writing` MEANS
    `rr 545-547 -- must I serve a statement of loss and damage` `my claim is for damages for personal injury or death` `the defendant waived compliance in writing`

@export By when must I serve my statement of loss and damage (r 547(1) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 28 days after the close of pleadings, and in any case before a request for trial date is filed.
GIVEN `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the pleadings close (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I serve my statement of loss and damage?` `the day the pleadings closed` MEANS
    `r 547(1) -- the last day for my statement of loss and damage` `the day the pleadings closed`

@export By when must the defendant serve its statement of expert and economic evidence on me (r 550(2) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 28 days after it is served with my statement of loss and damage, and before a request for trial date is filed.
GIVEN `the day my statement was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your statement of loss and damage served on the defendant (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant serve its statement?` `the day my statement was served` MEANS
    `r 550(2) -- the last day for the defendant's statement` `the day my statement was served`

@export Were my statement of loss and damage and the defendant's statement of expert and economic evidence served in time (rr 547 and 550 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? A late statement can count against that party in costs (r 557).
GIVEN `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the pleadings close (ISO date)?
      `the day my statement was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you serve your statement of loss and damage (ISO date)?
      `the defendant has served its statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant served its statement of expert and economic evidence?
      `the day the defendant's statement was served` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was it served on you (ISO date)? Otherwise give any date.
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date (ISO date)?
GIVETH A `Statements answer`
`Were the statements served in time?` `the day the pleadings closed` `the day my statement was served` `the defendant has served its statement` `the day the defendant's statement was served` `today` MEANS
    `rr 547, 550 -- were the statements in time` `the day the pleadings closed` `the day my statement was served` `the defendant has served its statement` `the day the defendant's statement was served` `today`

@export If damages are to be assessed before I have served my statement of loss and damage, by when must I serve it (r 558(2) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? At least 21 days before the date set for the assessment (not in a Magistrates Court, and only if the defendant has an address for service).
GIVEN `the day set for the assessment` IS A DATE @desc On what day is the assessment of damages set (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I serve my statement before the assessment?` `the day set for the assessment` MEANS
    `r 558(2) -- the last day for my statement before an assessment` `the day set for the assessment`

@export By when must the defendant serve its statement of expert and economic evidence before an assessment of my damages, if it intends to appear (r 558(4) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? 7 days before the date set for the assessment.
GIVEN `the day set for the assessment` IS A DATE @desc On what day is the assessment of damages set (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant serve its statement before the assessment?` `the day set for the assessment` MEANS
    `r 558(4) -- the day for the defendant's statement before an assessment` `the day set for the assessment`

§§ `Settling: a claimant under a legal incapacity (rr 98, 359) -- only if I am a child or lack capacity`

@export Is a settlement or an accepted offer binding on me if I am a person under a legal incapacity, such as a child (rr 98 and 359 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Only if the court, or the public trustee under the Public Trustee Act 1978 s 59, approves it.
GIVEN `I am a person under a legal incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a person under a legal incapacity (a child, or an adult with impaired capacity for the proceeding)?
      `the court or the public trustee approved it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court, or the public trustee under the Public Trustee Act 1978 s 59, approved the settlement or offer?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my settlement binding on me?` `I am a person under a legal incapacity` `the court or the public trustee approved it` MEANS
    `rr 98, 359 -- my settlement is binding on me` `I am a person under a legal incapacity` `the court or the public trustee approved it`

§§ `Dropping my claim (rr 303, 304, 307)`

@export Can I discontinue (drop) my claim without the court's leave or the other parties' consent (rr 303 and 304 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Yes before I am served with the first defence, unless there is more than one plaintiff, a counterclaim against me, or I represent another person.
GIVEN `I represent another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you bring the claim as a representative of another person (e.g. a litigation guardian or representative party)?
      `I have been served with the first defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has any defendant served its defence on you?
      `there is more than one plaintiff` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there more than one plaintiff in the proceeding?
      `there is a counterclaim against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a defendant made a counterclaim against you?
GIVETH A `Discontinuance answer`
`Can I discontinue without leave?` `I represent another person` `I have been served with the first defence` `there is more than one plaintiff` `there is a counterclaim against me` MEANS
    `rr 303, 304 -- can I discontinue without leave` `I represent another person` `I have been served with the first defence` `there is more than one plaintiff` `there is a counterclaim against me`

@export What costs must I pay if I discontinue my claim (r 307 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Without leave, the defendant's costs up to the discontinuance and costs it causes; with the court's leave, whatever the court orders.
GIVEN `I discontinue with the court's leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you discontinuing with the court's leave (rather than by notice or with the other parties' consent)?
GIVETH A `Discontinuance costs`
`What costs if I discontinue?` `I discontinue with the court's leave` MEANS
    `r 307 -- the costs if I discontinue` `I discontinue with the court's leave`

§§ `Offers to settle (rr 355, 358, 360-362)`

@export What is the earliest day my offer to settle can close (r 355(1) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? The offer must stay open for a period ending not less than 14 days after the day it is served.
GIVEN `the day the offer was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was (or will) the offer be served (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the earliest closing day for my offer?` `the day the offer was served` MEANS
    `r 355(1) -- the earliest closing day of an offer` `the day the offer was served`

@export Can I withdraw my offer to settle (r 355 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Not while it is open without the court's leave; it lapses at the end of its period.
GIVEN `the offer's closing day` IS A DATE @desc What is the last day the offer is open for acceptance (ISO date)?
      `the day I want to withdraw` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you want to withdraw it (ISO date)?
      `the court gives leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court given (or will it give) leave to withdraw the offer?
GIVETH A `Withdrawal answer`
`Can I withdraw my offer?` `the offer's closing day` `the day I want to withdraw` `the court gives leave` MEANS
    `r 355 -- can I withdraw my offer` `the offer's closing day` `the day I want to withdraw` `the court gives leave`

@export Can I still accept the defendant's offer to settle (rr 355 and 358 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Yes by serving written notice of acceptance while it is open, even after rejecting it or counteroffering, unless the court has already given leave to withdraw it; the court cannot extend the time.
GIVEN `the offer's closing day` IS A DATE @desc What is the last day the offer is open for acceptance (ISO date)?
      `the day I serve my acceptance` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you serve your written notice of acceptance (ISO date)?
      `the court gave leave to withdraw it before that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court already decided to give the defendant leave to withdraw the offer?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I accept the offer?` `the offer's closing day` `the day I serve my acceptance` `the court gave leave to withdraw it before that day` MEANS
    `rr 355, 358 -- I can accept the offer` `the offer's closing day` `the day I serve my acceptance` `the court gave leave to withdraw it before that day`

@export What costs order follows an offer to settle that was not accepted, once my claim is decided (rr 360, 361, 361A and 362 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? My offer beaten or matched: indemnity costs for me after it; the defendant's offer not reached, or my claim dismissed: indemnity costs against me after it.
GIVEN `who made it` IS A `Who made the offer` @desc Who made the offer? One of: "I made the offer to the defendant", "the defendant made the offer to me".
      `the offer was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offer accepted?
      `my proceeding was dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your claim dismissed?
      `the amount offered` IS A NUMBER @desc How much was the offer for, in Australian dollars?
      `the amount of my judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc How much was your judgment for, in Australian dollars, leaving out interest for the period after the day the offer was served?
      `the court is satisfied the offeror was willing and able to carry it out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that whoever made the offer was at all times willing and able to carry it out?
      `the offer was served on or after the first day of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the defendant's offer served on or after the first day of the trial or hearing?
      `the other side shows another costs order is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the party against whom the rule works shown the court another costs order is appropriate?
GIVETH AN `Offer costs answer`
`What costs follow the offer?` `who made it` `the offer was accepted` `my proceeding was dismissed` `the amount offered` `the amount of my judgment` `the court is satisfied the offeror was willing and able to carry it out` `the offer was served on or after the first day of the trial` `the other side shows another costs order is appropriate` MEANS
    `rr 360-362 -- the costs consequence of the offer` `who made it` `the offer was accepted` `my proceeding was dismissed` `the amount offered` `the amount of my judgment` `the court is satisfied the offeror was willing and able to carry it out` `the offer was served on or after the first day of the trial` `the other side shows another costs order is appropriate`

§§ `Delay (r 389) -- only if my case has stalled`

@export What must I do before taking the next step in a claim that has stalled (r 389 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? After a year with no step, give every other party a month's notice; after two years, get the court's order.
GIVEN `the day the last step was taken` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the last step taken in the proceeding by any party (ISO date)? An application on which no order was made does not count.
      `the day I want to take the next step` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you want to take your next step (ISO date)?
GIVETH A `Delay answer`
`What must I do before my next step?` `the day the last step was taken` `the day I want to take the next step` MEANS
    `r 389 -- what must I do before my next step` `the day the last step was taken` `the day I want to take the next step`

@export If I must give a month's notice of my intention to proceed, from what day can I take the step (r 389(1) of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? From the day after the month ends.
GIVEN `the day I gave notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you give the notice to every other party (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`From when can I take my step after notice?` `the day I gave notice` MEANS
    `r 389(1) -- the first day I can take the step after notice` `the day I gave notice`

§§ `Small claims in a Magistrates Court (rr 514, 515)`

@export Do the simplified procedures for minor claims apply to my claim in a Queensland Magistrates Court (rr 514-515 and Schedule 3 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Yes for a claim of not more than $25,000 including interest (not a QCAT minor civil dispute debt), or to the extent the parties agree in writing; then no disclosure unless ordered, and the court is not bound by the rules of evidence.
GIVEN `the amount I claim, including interest, after set-offs, payments and any excess I abandon` IS A NUMBER @desc How much do you claim, in Australian dollars, including interest, after any admitted set-off, amounts paid or credited, and any excess you abandon?
      `it is a QCAT minor civil dispute liquidated demand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a claim for a debt or liquidated demand of a kind that is a minor civil dispute for QCAT (QCAT Act, schedule 3, paragraph 1(a))?
      `the parties agreed in writing and filed the agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties agreed in writing to the simplified procedures and filed the agreement?
GIVETH A `Simplified procedures answer`
`Do the simplified procedures apply?` `the amount I claim, including interest, after set-offs, payments and any excess I abandon` `it is a QCAT minor civil dispute liquidated demand` `the parties agreed in writing and filed the agreement` MEANS
    `rr 514, Schedule 3 -- do the simplified procedures apply` `the amount I claim, including interest, after set-offs, payments and any excess I abandon` `it is a QCAT minor civil dispute liquidated demand` `the parties agreed in writing and filed the agreement`

§§ `Costs (rr 691, 697, Schedule 2)`

@export Which column of the Magistrates Courts scale of costs applies to my costs (r 691(3)-(4) and Schedule 2 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? On the standard basis the amount I recover decides it, on the indemnity basis the amount I claim; over $50,000, Part 3 of the scale.
GIVEN `the basis` IS A `Basis of costs` @desc On what basis are the costs to be assessed? One of: "the standard basis", "the indemnity basis".
      `the amount I recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc How much did you recover, in Australian dollars?
      `the amount I claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc How much did you claim, in Australian dollars?
GIVETH A `Magistrates scale column`
`Which Magistrates Courts scale column applies?` `the basis` `the amount I recovered` `the amount I claimed` MEANS
    `r 691, Schedule 2 -- the Magistrates Courts scale column` `the basis` `the amount I recovered` `the amount I claimed`

@export Are my costs cut back because I sued in a higher court than I needed to (r 697 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? If a lower court could have given my judgment when I began, my costs are assessed as if I had started there, unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc Which court did you sue in? One of: "the Supreme Court or the District Court", "a Magistrates Court".
      `I sued in the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you sue in the Supreme Court (not the District Court)?
      `a Magistrates Court could have given my judgment when I began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you started, could a Magistrates Court have given the judgment you got (under that court's own limits)?
      `the District Court could have given my relief when I began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you started, could the District Court have given all the relief you got?
GIVETH A `Higher court costs answer`
`Are my costs cut back for the court I chose?` `the court` `I sued in the Supreme Court` `a Magistrates Court could have given my judgment when I began` `the District Court could have given my relief when I began` MEANS
    `r 697 -- are my costs cut back for suing in a higher court` `the court` `I sued in the Supreme Court` `a Magistrates Court could have given my judgment when I began` `the District Court could have given my relief when I began`

§§ `Appeals (r 748)`

@export By when must I file a notice of appeal to the Queensland Court of Appeal (r 748 of the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999)? Within 28 days after the date of the decision, unless the Court of Appeal orders otherwise; then serve it as soon as practicable.
GIVEN `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the decision you want to appeal given (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I file my notice of appeal?` `the day of the decision` MEANS
    `r 748 -- the last day to file my notice of appeal` `the day of the decision`
